WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.983
Many years ago the king of Greece had a beautiful wife named Helen.

2
00:00:04.984 --> 00:00:13.816
History remembers her as the face that launched a thousand ships because the most heroic war of the ancient world was fought over her.

3
00:00:13.817 --> 00:00:19.150
Greece's great rival was the kingdom of Troy, across the Aegean Sea.

4
00:00:19.151 --> 00:00:24.711
The king of Troy had a son named Paris who fell in love with Helen and stole her away.

5
00:00:24.712 --> 00:00:29.038
The Greeks demanded that Helen be returned, but the Trojans refused.

6
00:00:29.039 --> 00:00:33.102
So the Greek army set sail in a thousand ships to attack.

7
00:00:33.103 --> 00:00:39.847
The Greeks camped on the shores near Troy and battled the Trojans on the windy plains before the city.

8
00:00:39.848 --> 00:00:46.091
For ten long years, the Trojan War dragged on, but neither army could defeat the other.

9
00:00:46.092 --> 00:00:54.155
Finally the Greek king gathered his generals and said, "For ten years we have attacked the walls and gates of Troy,

10
00:00:54.156 --> 00:00:57.400
but our enemy has always beaten us back.

11
00:00:57.401 --> 00:01:03.076
We need a new strategy to help us conquer the city."

12
00:01:03.077 --> 00:01:06.983
A Greek general who was known for his cunning plans spoke up.

13
00:01:06.984 --> 00:01:11.325
"I have a plan! Let's pretend to sail home in our ships.

14
00:01:11.326 --> 00:01:15.663
The Trojans will think that we have given up and that the war is over."

15
00:01:15.664 --> 00:01:19.744
"And then what?" asked the king.

16
00:01:19.745 --> 00:01:24.612
The clever general explained the rest of his plan, and the king nodded his head.

17
00:01:24.613 --> 00:01:31.878
"Hmm, it is a daring strategy," he said, "but I believe it will work!"

18
00:01:31.879 --> 00:01:38.606
The next day the Greeks didn't attack, but set to work building a huge wooden horse.

19
00:01:38.607 --> 00:01:42.661
For weeks they labored by their ships and left the city alone.

20
00:01:42.662 --> 00:01:50.201
Finally when the towering horse was ready, a few brave soldiers climbed into its belly through a secret door.

21
00:01:50.202 --> 00:01:56.655
The statue was mounted on wheels, and the other soldiers rolled it up to the gates of Troy.

22
00:01:56.656 --> 00:02:00.599
They returned to their ships, and the Greek army sailed out of sight.

23
00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:05.758
When the Trojans saw what the Greeks had left, they weren't sure what to do.

24
00:02:05.759 --> 00:02:10.320
"Stay away from the horse. It's a trick!" Paris warned his father.

25
00:02:10.321 --> 00:02:16.813
After so many years of war, the Trojans couldn't believe that the Greeks had finally given up.

26
00:02:16.814 --> 00:02:22.774
"Hmm, you may be right," said the Trojan king. "We must be cautious."

27
00:02:22.775 --> 00:02:27.506
The huge horse stood outside the city gates while the soldiers inside waited.

28
00:02:27.507 --> 00:02:33.381
As the Trojans puzzled over what to do, a Trojan soldier brought a prisoner in front of the king.

29
00:02:33.382 --> 00:02:38.224
"Your Majesty," he said, "I caught this Greek near the city gates.

30
00:02:38.225 --> 00:02:42.351
He must be a spy. He should be put to death."

31
00:02:42.352 --> 00:02:46.329
"Oh no, spare my life, please!" begged the Greek.

32
00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:49.171
"I will tell you everything you want to know."

33
00:02:49.172 --> 00:02:54.347
The king agreed and asked him why the Greeks had left behind the enormous wooden statue.

34
00:02:54.348 --> 00:02:59.922
"Our army was exhausted," the Greek explained, "and wanted to retreat safely.

35
00:02:59.923 --> 00:03:04.431
They decided to offer you a great war trophy so that you would not pursue them.

36
00:03:04.432 --> 00:03:07.056
That's all I know."

37
00:03:07.057 --> 00:03:11.095
"So it is a war trophy then? Ha-ha-ha!

38
00:03:11.096 --> 00:03:17.514
The Greeks have given up! Bring the wooden horse inside!" ordered the king.

39
00:03:17.515 --> 00:03:27.565
"We have won the war!" the people of Troy declared, and both the soldiers and citizens began to celebrate as the wooden horse was dragged into the city.

40
00:03:27.566 --> 00:03:34.967
It was so wonderfully made that no one noticed the secret door that hid the Greek soldiers inside.

41
00:03:34.968 --> 00:03:40.699
No one noticed, as well, the captured Greek smiling as the Trojan soldiers got drunk.

42
00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:42.932
He was a spy, after all.

43
00:03:42.933 --> 00:03:48.018
All around him, the citizens and soldiers of Troy drank and sang late into the night.

44
00:03:48.019 --> 00:03:55.044
When they had all fallen asleep, the Greek spy quietly approached the horse and opened the hidden door in its belly.

45
00:03:55.045 --> 00:03:58.312
The Greek soldiers climbed out.

46
00:03:58.313 --> 00:04:05.234
They approached the drunken guards half-asleep by the city gates, quickly overpowered them, and pushed the gates open.

47
00:04:05.235 --> 00:04:11.188
The Greek ships had only pretended to sail away and had returned with the Greek army during the night.

48
00:04:11.189 --> 00:04:15.740
Greek soldiers poured through the gates and lit fires all over the city.

49
00:04:15.741 --> 00:04:21.891
"Fire! Fire!" the Trojans shouted, waking in surprise.

50
00:04:21.892 --> 00:04:27.369
"No!" they cried when they realized it was a surprise attack.

51
00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:31.743
Some ran away. Some fought bravely, but it was too late.

52
00:04:31.744 --> 00:04:34.780
The Greeks had finally conquered Troy.

53
00:04:34.781 --> 00:04:40.177
The story of the Trojan War belongs to the myths and legends of the ancient Greeks.

54
00:04:40.178 --> 00:04:47.280
It was written by the legendary Greek poet Homer, who lived almost three thousand years ago.

55
00:04:47.281 --> 00:04:53.894
Little is known about him, except that he was blind, and many scholars debate whether he really existed.

56
00:04:53.895 --> 00:04:59.815
Many doubted too that the city of Troy ever existed until, in the late nineteenth century,

57
00:04:59.816 --> 00:05:07.140
archaeologists discovered an ancient city from the time of Homer on the Aegean coast.

58
00:05:07.141 --> 00:05:22.500
A great battle may have been fought there, and who knows, perhaps it was destroyed forever by the trick of a huge wooden horse.

